{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Polling every 10 seconds for data that changes every 10 minutes</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A dashboard polls <code>/api/summary</code> every 10 seconds to stay fresh. The underlying numbers actually change maybe once every few minutes. Every single poll, whether the data moved or not, downloads the entire JSON body again, a few kilobytes, dozens of times a minute, almost all of it identical to what's already on screen. On a slow connection or a metered plan, that's real, wasted bandwidth spent re-fetching data the client already has.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The naive fix, cache the response for some TTL and skip the network entirely, trades one problem for another: pick the TTL too long and a real update sits stale on screen; pick it too short and you're back to over-fetching. Neither a live poll nor a blind cache actually answers the question that matters, has this specific resource changed since I last saw it, one of them always asks (wastefully), the other never does (riskily).
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      What's missing is a validation step that's cheap enough to do on every request but only pays the transfer cost when there's actually new data. That's a different shape of caching than a TTL: instead of guessing how long data stays fresh, the client asks the server directly, and the server answers honestly, ideally in a response that costs almost nothing to send when the answer is "no change."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>ETag + If-None-Match: validate freshness without re-downloading the body</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The first request to a resource gets back a normal <code>200 OK</code>, the full body, plus a response header called <code>ETag</code>, a short identifier for that exact version of the data (like <code>"abc123"</code>). The client remembers it. The next request sends that value back in a request header called <code>If-None-Match: "abc123"</code>. If the data hasn't changed, the server replies <code>304 Not Modified</code> with no body at all, just headers, and the client keeps using what it already has. If the data has changed, the server sends a normal <code>200</code> with the new body and a new ETag.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is conditional GET: the request is unconditionally sent (the client still has to ask), but the response is conditional on whether the server's current ETag matches the one the client already holds. A <code>304</code> response is deliberately body-less, the client's job on receiving one is to keep serving its cached copy and just note that it's still valid, not to treat the response as new data to render.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The real gain over TTL-based caching is that ETag validation is correct by construction rather than by tuning: the client never serves data staler than "since my last request," and it never re-transfers a body the server can prove is identical to what the client already has. A TTL is a bet on how often the data changes; an ETag is a direct check of whether it did.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>90% of polls came back empty, and nobody had to guess a TTL</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team's "recently viewed items" panel polls its API regularly and can't tolerate a real update sitting stale for more than a few seconds, so a long TTL-based cache was never an option. They added ETag support: the server hashes the response body into an ETag, the client stores the last ETag per endpoint and sends it as <code>If-None-Match</code> on the next poll. Once shipped, real-user monitoring showed about 90% of polls coming back as a <code>304</code> with an empty body instead of the full multi-kilobyte payload, without loosening the freshness guarantee at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The part that made this a clean win rather than a trade-off: nobody had to pick a cache duration and hope it matched real update frequency. The client always has data that's correct as of its most recent request, the same guarantee an uncached poll gives, but at a fraction of the transfer cost, because most of those requests now cost one round trip of headers instead of headers plus a full body.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The one thing they had to get right on the client was not assuming every response has a body: a naive <code>fetch()</code> wrapper that always tries to <code>.json()</code> the response will throw on a <code>304</code>, since there's nothing to parse. The fetch layer needed an explicit branch, check <code>response.status === 304</code> first, fall back to the last cached payload, and only parse the body on an actual <code>200</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

