{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Weak vs. strong validators, and the frontend's job on a 304</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      ETags come in two flavors: a plain <code>"abc123"</code> is a strong validator (byte-for-byte identical), and a <code>W/"abc123"</code> with a <code>W/</code> prefix is a weak validator (semantically equivalent, but not guaranteed byte-identical, fine for "should I re-render this" but not for things like resuming a partial download). Most application APIs that hash a JSON payload for freshness checks use weak ETags, since exact byte identity usually isn't the property that actually matters.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>Last-Modified</code> / <code>If-Modified-Since</code> is the older, lower-resolution sibling mechanism, second-level timestamp precision instead of a content hash, cheaper to compute when hashing the full response body isn't worth the cost. Servers can and do support both; a client only needs to send whichever validator the previous response actually provided.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      On the frontend, the easy mistake is a fetch wrapper that assumes every response has a parseable body: calling <code>.json()</code> unconditionally throws on a real <code>304</code>, which has no body by definition. The fetch layer needs to branch on <code>response.status === 304</code> before attempting to parse anything, and fall back to the last cached payload. It's also worth pairing this with <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code> deliberately, despite the name, that directive doesn't mean "don't cache," it means "cache, but always revalidate before use," which is exactly the semantics ETag validation is built to support cheaply.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

